    {
      "id": "s005",
      "title": "Passage 5",
      "text": "If your investments grow by four percent a year after inflation and fees, that four hundred thousand could become roughly one point zero seven million over twenty-five years, with no new contributions. That looks like permission to stop. But change the assumed growth to two percent after inflation and fees, and you finish with roughly six hundred and fifty-six thousand dollars. Same starting money. Same retirement date. Very different ending.",
      "settings": {
        "space": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS",
        "endpoint": "/generate_voice_clone",
        "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base",
        "model_size": "1.7B",
        "language": "English",
        "use_xvector_only": true,
        "reference": "Audio/ren_reference.wav",
        "transcript": "Audio/ren_reference.txt",
        "other_generation_parameters": "unchanged public Space defaults",
        "reference_sha256": "78de4bd45743554f15ce32d8db39deaf1c0b1a9a5cd69fabb6d637dff323ed1c",
        "transcript_sha256": "46de9b48af8de666aa7275169f1f95fab8a398de3198c7d2fa51edbe22fb8451"
      },
      "fingerprint": "a3f434b8a94165142a442eaeb075585a45a2dd804fd15aa920b7506f98f24ff3",
      "status": "pending",
      "attempts": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s006",
      "title": "Passage 6",
      "text": "Neither rate is a forecast. These are simplified calculations, and real markets won\u2019t deliver the same return every year. The lesson is that your answer depends heavily on the assumptions you put into the calculator. So before you reduce anything, ask three questions. First: have I actually priced the retirement I want? \u201cA million dollars sounds good\u201d is a feeling. A retirement budget is a plan. Think about housing, food, taxes, health insurance, travel, and the expenses that arrive occasionally but expensively.",
      "settings": {
        "space": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS",
        "endpoint": "/generate_voice_clone",
        "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base",
        "model_size": "1.7B",
        "language": "English",
        "use_xvector_only": true,
        "reference": "Audio/ren_reference.wav",
        "transcript": "Audio/ren_reference.txt",
        "other_generation_parameters": "unchanged public Space defaults",
        "reference_sha256": "78de4bd45743554f15ce32d8db39deaf1c0b1a9a5cd69fabb6d637dff323ed1c",
        "transcript_sha256": "46de9b48af8de666aa7275169f1f95fab8a398de3198c7d2fa51edbe22fb8451"
      },
      "fingerprint": "2ea56dcea0da3f6d1c73edbab9a0e179ea8cd8e65e7f9360fe0f64b26cc6b12d",
      "status": "pending",
      "attempts": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s007",
      "title": "Passage 7",
      "text": "A paid-off mortgage doesn\u2019t pay your property taxes or replace your roof. Then estimate what Social Security or other dependable income could cover, and when it begins. Your investments need to cover the remaining gap. If you want to retire early, include the years before those payments start, plus how you\u2019ll fund spending before retirement accounts are readily accessible. And keep inflation consistent. A target expressed in today\u2019s dollars needs growth assumptions that account for inflation too.",
      "settings": {
        "space": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS",
        "endpoint": "/generate_voice_clone",
        "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base",
        "model_size": "1.7B",
        "language": "English",
        "use_xvector_only": true,
        "reference": "Audio/ren_reference.wav",
        "transcript": "Audio/ren_reference.txt",
        "other_generation_parameters": "unchanged public Space defaults",
        "reference_sha256": "78de4bd45743554f15ce32d8db39deaf1c0b1a9a5cd69fabb6d637dff323ed1c",
        "transcript_sha256": "46de9b48af8de666aa7275169f1f95fab8a398de3198c7d2fa51edbe22fb8451"
      },
      "fingerprint": "ab59656590cd70838a31560b1fb08313bbaecef215ba22fd3a7286ecce197136",
      "status": "pending",
      "attempts": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s008",
      "title": "Passage 8",
      "text": "Second: does the plan still work when life is less cooperative? Try lower returns. Higher spending. A longer retirement. A period when you can\u2019t contribute. If one slightly worse assumption breaks everything, you haven\u2019t found much room to ease off yet. And check your position outside retirement accounts. Do you have an emergency reserve? Is expensive debt under control? Would one broken furnace send you straight to a credit card? Retirement progress matters. So does being financially stable this year.",
      "settings": {
        "space": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS",
        "endpoint": "/generate_voice_clone",
        "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base",
        "model_size": "1.7B",
        "language": "English",
        "use_xvector_only": true,
        "reference": "Audio/ren_reference.wav",
        "transcript": "Audio/ren_reference.txt",
        "other_generation_parameters": "unchanged public Space defaults",
        "reference_sha256": "78de4bd45743554f15ce32d8db39deaf1c0b1a9a5cd69fabb6d637dff323ed1c",
        "transcript_sha256": "46de9b48af8de666aa7275169f1f95fab8a398de3198c7d2fa51edbe22fb8451"
      },
      "fingerprint": "bc50b530f0c54fdc50e08d8091f30682b8ec6001d5de457434717551969a10e0",
      "status": "pending",
      "attempts": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s009",
      "title": "Passage 9",
      "text": "Third: what would saving less actually give me? This is where the spreadsheet needs to meet your life. Maybe it buys a family trip while your parents can join you. Maybe it pays for help at home, or gives you room to work fewer hours. Or maybe it disappears into subscriptions you forget you have. You\u2019re deciding what the next dollar is for. Give it a specific job.",
      "settings": {
        "space": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS",
        "endpoint": "/generate_voice_clone",
        "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base",
        "model_size": "1.7B",
        "language": "English",
        "use_xvector_only": true,
        "reference": "Audio/ren_reference.wav",
        "transcript": "Audio/ren_reference.txt",
        "other_generation_parameters": "unchanged public Space defaults",
        "reference_sha256": "78de4bd45743554f15ce32d8db39deaf1c0b1a9a5cd69fabb6d637dff323ed1c",
        "transcript_sha256": "46de9b48af8de666aa7275169f1f95fab8a398de3198c7d2fa51edbe22fb8451"
      },
      "fingerprint": "2b89f2d39b3649e0e326dfd28433ff445b7baff4b0511f689a67ffbb6ebec75f",
      "status": "pending",
      "attempts": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s010",
      "title": "Passage 10",
      "text": "Also, reducing a pre-tax 401(k) contribution by five hundred dollars won\u2019t usually put five hundred dollars into your spending account. Taxes take a share. Check the actual change in take-home pay before making plans. Here\u2019s how I\u2019d turn those answers into a decision. Run your current contribution level through a retirement planner. Then run a lower level through the same assumptions, including the less favorable scenarios. What changes? Your retirement date? Your future spending? Your margin for error?",
      "settings": {
        "space": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS",
        "endpoint": "/generate_voice_clone",
        "model": "Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base",
        "model_size": "1.7B",
        "language": "English",
        "use_xvector_only": true,
        "reference": "Audio/ren_reference.wav",
        "transcript": "Audio/ren_reference.txt",
        "other_generation_parameters": "unchanged public Space defaults",
        "reference_sha256": "78de4bd45743554f15ce32d8db39deaf1c0b1a9a5cd69fabb6d637dff323ed1c",
